{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ולשומרי מצותי", :translit "u-leshomrei mitzvotai", :gloss "and to those who keep My commandments"} {:he "לא תשא", :translit "lo tissa", :gloss "you shall not take [the name]"} {:he "שלא לשם שמים", :translit "she-lo l'shem shamayim", :gloss "not for the sake of Heaven"} {:he "שם שמים לבטלה", :translit "shem shamayim la-vatalah", :gloss "the name of Heaven in vain"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikh ha-parshiyot: juxtaposition of adjacent verses to derive a legal or ethical inference."} {:kind :other, :note "Alternative reading: one who swears to fulfill a commandment must not take God's name in vain in that oath."}], :frame {:text "The principle that a commandment performed not for its own sake (she-lo lishmah) is spiritually deficient appears in multiple rabbinic sources. The specific analogy to taking God's name in vain is an application of the broader rabbinic valuation of intention in performance of mitzvot (Berakhot 17a, Pesachim 50b). The practice of swearing to fulfill commandments is itself grounded in the verse 'I have sworn and I will fulfill it' (Psalms 119:106), understood by the Sifre on Deuteronomy as referring to the oath taken at Sinai to keep the commandments (Sifrei Deuteronomy 296).", :sources ["Berakhot 17a" "Pesachim 50b" "Psalms 119:106" "Sifrei Deuteronomy 296"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikh ha-parshiyot: juxtaposition of adjacent verses to derive a legal or ethical inference.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Alternative reading: one who swears to fulfill a commandment must not take God's name in vain in that oath.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:34:33.188691193Z", :model "glm-5.2"}